claude-rag-hook (0.3.3-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: indexer now emits throttled per-file progress messages
    during the embed loop, so the .progress file actually reflects
    live state. Before this, files_done was set to 0 at start and
    never updated until the run finished, which meant bare `rag`
    on a real repo (3000+ files) reported "0/3815 files" for the
    entire 30-minute run and looked wedged. The throttle is "every
    16 files OR every 2 seconds, whichever fires first" so the
    progress file isn't rewritten thousands of times per second.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 14:50:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.3.2-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: fastembed model cache no longer lives in /tmp, where it gets
    wiped on every reboot (and intermittently by systemd-tmpfiles
    during uptime), forcing a 4-minute re-download from HuggingFace.
    The hook now points fastembed at /var/cache/claude-rag-hook/models/
    (machine-wide, created by apt postinst, mode 2775 group adm so
    any local user can read and the first user fills the shared
    cache for everyone). Falls back to ~/.cache/claude-rag-hook/models/
    if /var/cache is unwritable (rare; e.g. read-only host).
  * Fix: when the on-disk model cache exists but the .onnx file is
    missing (the /tmp purge case, or any interrupted download),
    fastembed used to throw NoSuchFile and the indexer would
    persist a "failed" .progress that required manual intervention.
    The hook now detects this state, wipes the broken model dir,
    and lets fastembed re-download cleanly. Plus a self-heal retry
    on any TextEmbedding construction error.
  * Install: apt postinst now best-effort pre-fetches the default
    embedding model (~80MB ONNX) into /var/cache so the first
    `rag <q>` is fast on a fresh install. Pre-fetch is silent on
    failure (network down, fastembed not installed, etc.); the
    runtime auto-recovery path covers all the same cases on demand.
  * Install: apt purge now removes /var/cache/claude-rag-hook/. A
    plain `apt remove` leaves the cache so a reinstall is fast.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 14:00:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * UX: bare `rag` now uses Claude Code's documented `decision: "block"`
    envelope so the model is not invoked. The user sees the status
    text directly; no tokens spent, no Claude paraphrase, no
    cross-purpose advice from a confused model. (In v0.3.0 the status
    text leaked into the prompt and Claude tried to relay it.)
  * UX: bare `rag` in a folder with no index yet now kicks off the
    indexer immediately. A single `rag` is enough to get setup
    started; you no longer have to invent a throwaway query for the
    first invocation.
  * UX: `rag <q>` against a not-yet-indexed folder persists the query
    as a "queued" replay hint. Once indexing finishes, the next
    prompt the user submits surfaces a one-time banner ("indexing
    complete; your earlier `rag <q>` is ready - type that again to
    use it") so the user does not have to remember what they asked.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 07:30:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * UX: bare `rag` (or `/rag`, `rag status`, `rag:`) is now a status
    command. Reports index location, ready/indexing/error state, files
    and chunks counts, last-run timing, indexer log path. Output goes
    to both stderr (compact terminal line) and stdout (verbose status
    block) so Claude can answer follow-up questions about the index.
    Never blocks, never runs retrieval.
  * Reliability: retrieval is now wall-clock-capped at
    `retrieval.timeout_seconds` (default 8s). Cold-start fastembed
    model loads on the first call after boot used to hold Claude for
    tens of seconds with no signal; the hook now gives up cleanly,
    falls through, and tells the user what happened. Retry usually
    succeeds in 1-3s.
  * Discoverability: when the hook fork-detaches an indexer, the stderr
    nudge now ends with "type `rag` (alone) any time to check
    progress" so users who miss the initial message can rediscover it.
  * Awareness: when a non-rag prompt is submitted while a background
    indexer is still running, the hook prepends a small "[claude-rag-hook]
    heads-up: still indexing..." banner to stdout so Claude (and the
    user) know the detached job is still working.
  * Notifications: on first-time-index completion, the hook fires a
    desktop notification via notify-send if it is on PATH. Refreshes
    stay quiet. Disable with `notifications.on_index_complete: false`.
  * Run stats: indexer now writes a `.last_run.json` next to the
    `.progress` file so `rag` (status) can report concrete numbers.
  * Docs: README + Pages site updated. New "How it works under the
    hood" section explains synchronous-hook timing, fork-detach
    behavior, where progress and logs live. Hydra-llm interop section
    expanded to spell out independence of indexes between the two
    tools.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 06:45:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: auto-index incorrectly refused direct children of $HOME, even
    those with project markers. Folders like ~/regurio-monorepo,
    ~/projects/foo, ~/code/bar are common and legitimate places to
    keep a project; refusing them out of an abundance of caution
    silently broke the integration ('rag:' looked like it did
    nothing). The project-marker requirement and the size cap already
    gate everything that needs gating; the redundant rule is dropped.
    $HOME itself is still refused.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Thu, 07 May 2026 22:50:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Major redesign: integration tool, not CLI.
  * Drop the user-facing CLI entirely (no more index/query/ls/rm/config/
    daemon/install/uninstall/version subcommands; no /usr/bin/claude-rag-hook
    on $PATH). The package now ships a Claude Code hook and the small
    pieces it needs, all under /usr/lib/claude-rag-hook/. End users
    install the .deb and use Claude Code normally; nothing on the
    command line for them to learn.
  * Wire into Claude Code automatically. The apt postinst merges the
    hook entry into /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json (the
    documented system-wide settings file Claude Code reads with the
    highest precedence for every user on the machine). Every Claude
    Code session, current and future user, picks up the hook on next
    start without per-user setup.
  * Auto-index on first 'rag:' in a project folder. The hook
    fork-detaches a background indexer for the nearest project root
    (the ancestor with .git, pyproject.toml, package.json, Cargo.toml,
    go.mod, etc.); the user's current prompt passes through to Claude
    unchanged so they get an answer right now. The next 'rag:' uses
    the populated index. Subsequent 'rag:' turns trigger an
    incremental refresh in the background, throttled to 5 minutes.
  * Auto-index safety rails: hard refusal on $HOME and direct
    children, /, /etc, /var, /tmp, /usr, /opt, /root, /boot, /sys,
    /proc, /dev. Project-marker required (a .git or similar) within
    six ancestors of cwd. Size cap: 20k files / 500 MB; bypass via
    CLAUDE_RAG_HOOK_BYPASS_SIZE_CAP=1.
  * Fix settings.json schema. The previous 0.1.0 entry used a fake
    "name" field at the top level of each matcher; Claude Code's real
    schema is {"hooks":[{"type":"command","command":"..."}]} nested
    inside the matcher, no top-level name. uninstall now finds our
    entry by command-equality on the absolute hook path.
  * Drop the <context></context> wrapper from hook output. Per Claude
    Code's documented contract, plain stdout from a UserPromptSubmit
    hook is appended to the prompt as a system reminder; tag wrapping
    is unnecessary.
  * Postinst is idempotent and merges into existing managed-settings.json
    (other admins / other tools' entries are preserved). Postrm
    removes only the entry whose command matches our hook path.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Thu, 07 May 2026 22:00:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Initial release.
  * UserPromptSubmit hook subcommand: parses 'rag:', '/rag', and
    'rag@<tag>:' trigger forms, walks up from cwd to find a
    .claude-rag-index/ (also reads hydra-llm's .hydra-index/), embeds
    the query, retrieves top-K chunks, and emits a context block on
    stdout for Claude Code to prepend to the user's prompt.
  * Indexer: gitignore-aware walker, code/prose classifier, line-aware
    overlap chunker (1500-char target, 200-char overlap), incremental
    refresh keyed on (mtime, size).
  * Storage: per-folder LanceDB at <folder>/.claude-rag-index/, with a
    meta.yaml recording the embedder used and a files.json driving
    incremental refresh.
  * Embedder backends: fastembed (default, pure-Python ONNX),
    OpenAI-compatible /v1/embeddings client, hydra-llm interop.
  * Warm embedder daemon over a per-user Unix domain socket; idle TTL
    so it does not linger when not in use.
  * CLI: install / uninstall / index / query / ls / rm / config /
    daemon {status,start,stop} / hook / version.
  * install / uninstall patch ~/.claude/settings.json with a clear
    backup; an entry tagged 'name: claude-rag-hook' is the unit of
    install/uninstall so it does not interfere with other hooks.
  * Disclaimer printed on every CLI invocation (suppress with
    CLAUDE_RAG_HOOK_NO_DISCLAIMER=1) and embedded in --help.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Thu, 07 May 2026 18:00:00 +0000
